Z||000003||Old Lar||11-03-2000||08:43 PM||||Unfortunately, many of the store managers at places like BP, or various mini marts and retail outlets do not necessarily have the smarts or skills to be management material.  Their jobs are also made difficult in trying to staff a store that needs 24 hour coverage with some people who are not that reliable.  Their job is to staff the store with the correct number of people with some who may not show up, or fill the time slot themselves.<P>This is not a new phenomena.  It was like that when I started at entry levels jobs in the mid 60's.  Some scheduled workers would not show up for work.  So, if there was enough time, the managers would try to schedule others. Conflict of your time commitments.  And if you are a good worker, you get called.<P>Now in todays low unemployment levels, we have flex time. People come and go as they please.  Companies are expected to adapt to needs of the individual, which makes life at work a lot of fun.  There are programs for management to hire, retain and promote diversity (minority)candidates, many really take advantage of the system.  Management will do nothing that will jeprodize their position with the minority staffing levels.  Got off topic for a bit.<P>I would not expect the situation at BP to improve.  You do the math and try to figure out a 24 hour schedule with people who want to pick and choose part time hours of work.  It is a scheduling nightmare. You will need to determine if $1.10 pay cut will be worth the decrease drive time and wear and tear on the Fiero with the expected frustration of a screwed up work schedule.  I would not like to be in that position today.  I don't have a life outside of work.  I'm expected to backfill for the ones who don't show up.||||reg||9
